Yep. This wraps up the Windows Live Investigation portion nicely. The new concept you really need from this section is Differential Analysis. The rest is mostly consolidating the PowerShell commands you've already learned.

1. Differential Analysis: the big new concept

The problem is simple:

A Windows machine might have:

Hundreds of processes
Hundreds of services
Lots of scheduled tasks
Many network connections
Huge numbers of registry entries

Looking manually at everything is inefficient.

Instead, ask:

What's different from what this machine is supposed to look like?

That's differential analysis.

Simple example

When the machine was originally built:

Baseline / Gold Image
---------------------
Service A
Service B
Service C


During the incident:

Current Machine
---------------
Service A
Service B
Service C
weirdService


Instead of reviewing every service individually, comparison immediately highlights:

+ weirdService


Now you have something to investigate.

2. Baseline vs current state

SEC504 shows something along these lines:

Get-Service > baseline-services.txt


That's your known reference.

During investigation:

Get-Service > services-liveinvestigation.txt


Load both:

$baseline = Get-Content .\baseline-services.txt
$current = Get-Content .\services-liveinvestigation.txt


Then:

Compare-Object -ReferenceObject $baseline -DifferenceObject $current


Now PowerShell shows what's different.

3. SideIndicator is the exam-worthy bit

This can initially look confusing.

Remember:

<=   Reference / baseline side

=>   Difference / current side


The slide gives a really good memory trick:

The arrow points toward the side containing that entry.

So:

SuspiciousService   =>


means it's on the current/difference side.

That's potentially something that appeared since the baseline.

Whereas:

KnownService        <=


is on the reference/baseline side but not in the current output in the same form.

🧠 Memory trick

Baseline ←

<=


Current →

=>


Easy.

4. Why this is powerful for IR

Imagine the attacker establishes persistence using:

MicrosoftUpdateManager


The name deliberately looks legitimate.

If you simply enumerate services you might see hundreds:

Windows Update
VMware Tools
Defender
SQL Agent
MicrosoftUpdateManager
...


You might miss it.

But comparison against the gold image shows:

MicrosoftUpdateManager  =>


Now you've got a lead.

It's the change that's interesting, not necessarily the name.

5. Differential analysis isn't just for services

This is an important conceptual extension.

You can use the baseline/current approach for things such as:

Processes

What's running now that isn't normally present?

Services

What services appeared/disappeared/changed?

Scheduled tasks

What unexpected task exists?

Local accounts/groups

Did an unexpected account or administrator appear?

Network listeners

Is an unexpected port listening?

Configuration

What relevant system setting changed?

So the concept is broader than:

Compare-Object command.

The real concept is:

Known-good baseline + current state → investigate meaningful differences.

6. What if there is no baseline?

SEC504 makes a practical point here.

If you never collected baseline information, you may sometimes be able to provision a clean system from the same gold image and collect reference information from that.

Conceptually:

Known clean build
        ↓
Collect configuration
        ↓
Compare
        ↓
Compromised/suspected host


Of course the comparison is only meaningful if the reference configuration actually represents what the investigated machine was supposed to look like.

7. PowerShell vs legacy commands

SEC504 also explicitly says:

It's okay to use legacy Windows commands.

You don't need to force PowerShell for every problem.

For exam revision, this mapping is useful:

Goal	PowerShell	LegacyProcesses	Get-Process	wmic.exe process
Network connections	Get-NetTCPConnection	netstat.exe -nao
Services	Get-Service	sc.exe query
Registry	Get-ChildItem	reg.exe
Local users	Get-LocalUser	net.exe user
Local groups	Get-LocalGroup	net.exe localgroup
Scheduled tasks	Get-ScheduledTask	schtasks.exe
Event logs	Get-WinEvent	wevtutil.exe
Compare data	Compare-Object	fc.exe
Small but useful SEC504 tip

When running legacy programs from PowerShell, specify .exe.

For example:

netstat.exe -nao


rather than simply relying on:

netstat


This avoids confusion with PowerShell aliases with overlapping names.

8. Sysinternals ⭐

This part is worth remembering because these tools come up constantly in Windows investigation.

Process Explorer

Think:

Supercharged Task Manager

Detailed examination of running processes.

Useful mental connection:

Processes → Process Explorer

Process Monitor / Procmon

Think:

What's happening right now?

It can show activity involving areas such as:

Processes
File system
Registry
Network

So:

Process Monitor = activity over time


while:

Process Explorer = detailed process examination


Don't confuse those two.

9. TCPView

This directly relates to what you just learned with:

Get-NetTCPConnection


Think:

Network connection/listener → owning application/process

So instead of looking only at:

192.168.1.50 → 123.45.67.89:443


you can correlate the network activity to the responsible application.

10. Autoruns ⭐

This one is extremely valuable for IR.

Think:

What is configured to start automatically?

The slide describes Autoruns as providing a comprehensive view of Windows AutoStart Extensibility Points (ASEPs).

The security relevance should now be obvious:

Attacker wants persistence
           ↓
Something executes automatically
           ↓
Autoruns can help reveal autostart configuration


This ties together several things you've already studied:

Services
Registry
Scheduled execution
Other autostart mechanisms
11. Sysmon ⭐

Sysmon is different.

Think:

Detailed Windows security telemetry collection

The slide describes it as a service/driver that records detailed event information that becomes particularly valuable with monitoring such as a SIEM or Windows Event Collection.

This is more Prepare/Detect visibility than a one-time investigation tool.

And you'll immediately recognize the relevance to MDR:

Endpoint activity
       ↓
Sysmon telemetry
       ↓
Central collection/SIEM
       ↓
Detection/Hunting

12. ProcDump

SEC504 describes ProcDump as a command-line utility for capturing memory from a running process for later analysis.

The important thing to remember:

It's dual-use.

Responders may use tools like this for analysis, while threat actors may abuse legitimate administrative/debugging utilities.

That reinforces another recurring SEC504 lesson:

Tool name alone does not establish intent. Context and behavior matter.

🔥 Now connect the WHOLE Windows Live Investigation module

This is the structure I'd memorize instead of memorizing paragraphs.

PROCESS

What's running?

Get-Process


More details:

Get-CimInstance -Class Win32_Process


Look at:

Name
Path
Command line
PID
Parent PID
Parent/child relationships
NETWORK

Who's communicating?

Get-NetTCPConnection


Look at:

Listening ports
Established connections
Remote IP
Remote port
Owning process
Unexpected/beacon-like behavior
SERVICES

Is something persistently running?

Get-Service


More detail:

Get-CimInstance -ClassName Win32_Service


Look especially at the service executable/configuration.

REGISTRY

Has interesting configuration/persistence changed?

Get-ChildItem
Get-ItemProperty

ACCOUNTS

Did someone add or privilege an account?

Get-LocalUser
Get-LocalGroup
Get-LocalGroupMember Administrators

SCHEDULED TASKS

Is something configured to execute later/automatically?

Get-ScheduledTask
Export-ScheduledTask
Get-ScheduledTaskInfo

EVENT LOGS

What did Windows record?

Get-WinEvent


From the previous material, remember the example:

System Event ID 7045 → service installation

DIFFERENTIAL ANALYSIS

What's different from our known-good state?

Compare-Object


Remember:

<= Baseline/reference

=> Current/difference

