🎯 How it applies to an actual investigation

Suppose tomorrow you encounter:

Unknown.exe
→ unusual external destination


Your SEC504 brain should now naturally expand that lead:

Unknown.exe
       ↓
PROCESS
Who launched it?
Command line?
Path?
       ↓
NETWORK
Who's it talking to?
Listening?
Beaconing?
       ↓
SERVICES
Does a service persist it?
       ↓
REGISTRY
Autostart configuration?
       ↓
ACCOUNTS
Unexpected users/admins?
       ↓
TASKS
Scheduled execution?
       ↓
EVENT LOGS
When/how did relevant changes occur?
       ↓
DIFFERENTIAL ANALYSIS
What's different from known-good?
       ↓
NEW IOC/TTP FOUND
       ↓
DAIR → SCOPE AGAIN 🔄


That's the real lesson of this module.

You aren't hunting "evil.exe". You're reconstructing what changed, what executed, what communicated, how activity persisted, what accounts were involved, and how far the activity may extend.

That mindset will be much more valuable for your GCIH exam, and it also maps almost perfectly to the way you're already doing Microsoft Defender XDR hunts.
